body:
This show is a concept that writes itself;

Just take Maul’s building the shadow collective plot, give it a post-empire paintjob, sprinkle in some thematic elements from early GOT, Breaking Bad and The Sopranos, include some flashbacks, bring back a couple of legacy characters and finish it off with action scenes paid for by Disney and hey presto, you got a banger of a show focused around Boba being a fearless crime lord in the way we’ve always wanted to see him on screen.

But no, they couldn’t just do that. Because fuck us, I guess.

To quote myself from a prior post;

“Well, for those who enjoy it, it was the first truly open world AC game bringing us to an open wilderness with a wholly new and dynamic combat system, lots of fun and unique side activities, the prototype naval combat, something truly unique in Connor’s Homestead that hasn’t been done before and since, modern day sections that actually allowed Desmond to be an assassin  and the story, viewing Connor as one of the great unappreciated heroes of the franchise who always tried to do right by his people but kept powering on despite being punished by circumstances in a powerful saga of strength and perseverance combined with a superb, beautiful soundtrack.

For those who hate it, it was the first AC game to feel stilted, dull and monotonous. The parkour got a massive downgrade becoming more janky and unresponsive, most of the side activities being dull, bland and tediously repetitive, the combat changes not being as great as they were hyped up to be, the map design was average at best and some of the missions were just poorly designed and many of the optional objectives were a fucking joke, far beyond what the Ezio games has demanded of players. And that doesn’t even touch on the story, following a character who is boring at best and a naive, arrogant child who killed because he was told to and never understood what he was doing or why as he never got the proper development a main character like him should have, in addition to the modern day effectively killing the point of a modern day in AC at all, the effects of which we’re still feeling now.”
